# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE DEPLOY CONTRACT FOR apps/app — every variable the app reads, and when.
#
# Two kinds, and the difference is the single most common way a first deploy
# fails:
#
#   BUILD-TIME (NEXT_PUBLIC_*): inlined into the bundle by `next build`. Setting
#     one in the Vercel dashboard AFTER a build changes NOTHING until the next
#     build. Set them for Production AND Preview before the first deploy.
#   RUNTIME (everything else): read by the server on each request. Missing means
#     a 500 on the first request that needs it — not at startup.
#
# `apps/app/scripts/deploy-preflight.mjs` runs before every `next build` and,
# ON A DEPLOYMENT BUILD ONLY (VERCEL=1), refuses to build if any of these is
# unset or carries a local value. It is silent in CI and on a developer's
# machine. So an incomplete deploy fails at BUILD, with the variable named,
# rather than at the first request with a generic error.
#
# ADDING A VARIABLE HERE MEANS ADDING IT IN THREE PLACES IN THE SAME COMMIT:
# this file, the preflight, AND turbo.json's `build.env`. Turborepo's strict
# env mode (the default) hands the build task ONLY the names turbo.json
# declares; the preflight runs inside that task and cannot see anything else,
# however it was set. Measured 2026-08-19 on the first real Vercel build: nine
# runtime variables set on the project, all nine reported "unset", because
# only the NEXT_PUBLIC_* names were declared. A variable missing from turbo.json
# is reported as missing from the deploy even when it is not.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── BUILD-TIME ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
# The `sb_publishable_…` key — Project Settings → API → "Publishable key". It
# is designed to ship inside the browser bundle; RLS is the security boundary,
# not this value. Named `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` and carrying the legacy
# `anon` JWT until 2026-08-19 — both the hosted platform and the local CLI issue
# the new form, supabase-js 2.112 handles it explicitly, and the legacy form
# stops working at the end of 2026.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# The origin `src/lib/api.ts`'s `apiGet` self-fetches against (a Server
# Component's server-side `/v1` call), WITH THE SESSION COOKIE ATTACHED.
#
# REQUIRED IN EVERY PRODUCTION BUILD, AND ON EVERY NON-LOOPBACK HOST. It may be
# left unset only by `next dev` on a loopback host — `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`,
# `[::1]`, `*.localhost`, any port. Anywhere else `resolveBaseOrigin` REFUSES to
# build a target and the page renders its error state.
#
# `next start` IS A PRODUCTION BUILD AND THEREFORE NEEDS IT, loopback or not —
# corrected 2026-08-17, this comment previously listed `next start` and
# `qa/field.mjs` among the cases that may leave it unset. The loopback fallback
# took its PORT from the request, so on a deployment that forgot this variable
# `Host: 127.0.0.1:9200` aimed the cookie-bearing self-fetch at an
# attacker-chosen local port. There is no header-derived origin in a production
# build now, for any host. `qa/field.mjs` sets it to its own ephemeral
# `next start` origin, which is also how the browser pass came to exercise the
# branch a real deployment uses.
#
# IT IS INLINED AT BUILD TIME for the client bundle, and read at runtime by the
# server ONLY IF the build left it unset (measured against `.next/server`
# output). A container built once with a value baked in ignores whatever the
# runtime environment says — so a deploy to a new origin needs a REBUILD, not
# just a changed variable.
#
# That is deliberate and this comment used to say the opposite ("Leave unset
# in every ordinary case"). The origin used to be assembled from the inbound
# `Host` header, which any client can set: a request carrying
# `Host: attacker.example` made this server fetch
# `https://attacker.example/v1/projects` with a real foreman's Supabase auth
# cookies on it. Nothing in this repository guarantees a proxy that normalises
# `Host`. Setting this value is how a real origin becomes trusted, and it is
# not derived from anything a request says.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ORIGIN=
# ── RUNTIME ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 'app_pw' is a LOCAL-ONLY dev password set by supabase/seed.sql (never by a
# migration, so a *default* `supabase db push` never applies it — but
# `--include-seed`, `db reset --linked --include-seed`, and Supabase
# Branching preview branches do apply seed.sql, so this value must never be
# used on any hosted/real project). goproceed_app membership is full
# tenant-table access — staging/prod must use a freshly generated secret,
# never this value. See infra/README-staging.md §3.
APP_DB_URL=postgresql://goproceed_app_login:app_pw@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres

# The server's own identity. Used only for writes the server makes on its own
# behalf — inspection verdicts and server-sourced capture events — so that a
# member who can execute SQL as goproceed_app cannot forge them. 'service_pw' is
# a LOCAL-ONLY dev password set by scripts/set-local-app-password.mjs, never by
# a migration or by seed.sql. Staging and production must use a freshly
# generated secret; see infra/README-staging.md §3.
SERVICE_DB_URL=postgresql://goproceed_service_login:service_pw@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres

# THE TWO THIS FILE DID NOT LIST UNTIL 2026-08-18, AND THE APP DEFAULTS THEM TO
# THE LOCAL STACK. `src/lib/evidence-storage.ts` reads both to issue signed
# upload URLs and to move bytes between the staging and originals prefixes of
# the `evidence` bucket. Unset, SUPABASE_URL falls back to
# http://127.0.0.1:54321 and the key to the published local demo secret — so a
# deployment that set only the documented variables would aim every evidence
# upload at a Supabase that does not exist on the server, and the first photo
# would be the first symptom. Not NEXT_PUBLIC_: the service-role key is a
# server secret and must never reach the browser (tenancy-and-security.md
# §"Browser, field client, and native").
#
# SUPABASE_URL is the same host as NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL. It is a separate
# variable because the server reads it without the NEXT_PUBLIC_ inlining
# machinery, and because keeping them separate is what lets the deploy
# preflight tell "the browser's Supabase" from "the server's" if they ever
# diverge (they must not).
SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
# The `sb_secret_…` key — Project Settings → API → "Secret keys" (NOT the legacy
# `service_role` JWT, which this variable was named for until 2026-08-19 and
# which stops working at the end of 2026). Local default is the CLI-issued demo
# secret in evidence-storage.ts and is NOT repeated here.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# ── v0.1-M5, the protected external link ─────────────────────────────────────
# The origin the bearer link points at. NOT derived from the request: a link
# built from a Host header is a link an attacker can aim at their own origin by
# sending one, and this same value is the ONLY Origin the exchange and the
# decision submit accept. Must be https in every environment except a developer's
# localhost.
#
# ONE CONSEQUENCE OF __Host- ON localhost: the session cookie carries Secure, and
# a browser refuses a Secure cookie over plain http. Integration tests drive the
# routes directly and are unaffected; clicking a link in a local browser over
# http is not going to work, and dropping the prefix in development would mean
# the thing under test is not the thing that ships.
EXTERNAL_LINK_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# Keyed verifiers, with key ids, so a key can be rotated without re-deriving
# verifiers that cannot be re-derived (the raw token is gone — INV-044).
# Format: <keyId>:<base64 secret>[,<keyId>:<base64 secret>…], secrets >= 32 bytes.
# The ACTIVE key signs new material; every key in the list can still verify.
# THERE IS NO DEFAULT AND THERE MUST NOT BE: a default key is a key in the source
# tree, which is a key in every deployment that forgot to set one. A deployment
# with no external key cannot issue a link, which is the correct behaviour.
# Generate with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
EXTERNAL_LINK_HMAC_KEYS=dev1:ZGV2LW9ubHktbGluay1rZXktMzItYnl0ZXMtbG9uZy0xMjM0
EXTERNAL_LINK_ACTIVE_KEY_ID=dev1
EXTERNAL_SESSION_HMAC_KEYS=dev1:ZGV2LW9ubHktc2Vzc2lvbi1rZXktMzItYnl0ZXMtbG9uZy0xMg
EXTERNAL_SESSION_ACTIVE_KEY_ID=dev1

# ── Plan C: cross-origin field client ─────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated exact origins that the `/v1` CORS layer accepts on `Origin:`.
# OPTIONAL — unset means the CORS layer is off and `/v1` behaves exactly as
# before. Set it only when Plan C's Expo-web field client origin exists. No
# wildcards or trailing slashes; scheme+host only. Example:
# https://goproceed-field.vercel.app
FIELD_CLIENT_ORIGINS=

# ── Telegram project channel (runtime/server-only) ─────────────────────────
# Never expose these values to the browser or commit real values. The adapter
# validates them lazily only when a Telegram operation is invoked.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_BOT_ID=
TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
TELEGRAM_WORKER_SECRET=
TELEGRAM_LINK_PEPPER=
APP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://app.example.com
